API patterns (also called API design patterns) are standard ways to design, expose, and consume APIs so they are scalable, maintainable,
and easy to use. Since you’re a Java backend developer, I’ll explain them in a clear, interview-oriented way with examples.

KEY IDEA (easy memory):
REST     -> work with RESOURCES (nouns)     e.g. /users/10
RPC      -> call ACTIONS (verbs)            e.g. /calculateTax
GraphQL  -> client ASKS for exact fields    e.g. { user { name } }
Event    -> fire & forget via MESSAGE BUS   e.g. OrderCreated → Kafka
Webhook  -> SERVER calls CLIENT on event    e.g. payment success callback
Gateway  -> ONE door for many services
BFF      -> ONE backend shape per frontend


================================================================================
QUICK COMPARISON (interview gold)
================================================================================
Pattern          Style              Sync/Async     Best for
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
REST             Resource URLs      Sync           Public/CRUD APIs, Spring Boot
CRUD             REST subset        Sync           Simple DB apps
RPC              Function call      Sync           Internal actions, gRPC
SOAP             XML + WSDL         Sync           Banks / legacy enterprise
GraphQL          Query language     Sync           Complex UI, mobile apps
Event-Driven     Topics/queues      Async          Microservices decoupling
Webhooks         HTTP callback      Async-ish      3rd-party notifications
API Gateway      Edge entry point   -              Auth, rate limit, routing
BFF              Per-frontend API   Sync           Mobile vs Web different needs
HATEOAS          Links in response  Sync           Hypermedia / discoverable APIs


WHEN TO USE WHICH (decision guide)
- Simple CRUD app / admin panel          -> REST + CRUD
- Need action like "sendOtp", "payNow"   -> RPC style endpoint (or REST command)
- Frontend needs flexible nested data    -> GraphQL
- Bank / strict contract / XML legacy    -> SOAP
- Services must not wait on each other   -> Event-Driven (Kafka/RabbitMQ)
- Notify external system after event     -> Webhooks
- Many microservices behind one URL      -> API Gateway
- Mobile needs smaller payload than web  -> BFF


================================================================================
1. REST (Representational State Transfer)
================================================================================

📌 Most common API pattern

Key ideas
- Resource-based URLs (nouns, not verbs)
- Uses HTTP methods
- Stateless (each request has all info; server does not store client session for API)
- Representation usually JSON

Example

GET    /users/10
POST   /users
PUT    /users/10
DELETE /users/10


HTTP methods meaning

GET    → read
POST   → create
PUT    → update (full replace of resource)
PATCH  → update (partial fields only)
DELETE → remove

PUT vs PATCH (common follow-up)
PUT  /users/10  { "name":"Ali", "email":"a@x.com", "age":25 }  -> send FULL object
PATCH /users/10 { "email":"new@x.com" }                        -> change only email

Idempotency (interview tip)
- Idempotent = same request many times → same result
- GET, PUT, DELETE → usually idempotent
- POST → usually NOT idempotent (calling twice can create 2 records)

Good URL vs Bad URL
✅ GET /users/10/orders
❌ GET /getUserOrders?id=10   (RPC style mixed into REST)

✅ Simple
✅ Cacheable (especially GET)
❌ Over-fetching / under-fetching sometimes

Used in: Spring Boot, Microservices


================================================================================
2. CRUD Pattern
================================================================================

📌 Built on REST, focused on database operations

Operation   HTTP Method
Create      POST
Read        GET
Update      PUT / PATCH
Delete      DELETE

Example

POST /orders
GET  /orders/123

✅ Easy to understand
❌ Not good for complex workflows (e.g. "approve order", "cancel & refund")
   For those, people often add RPC-like endpoints: POST /orders/123/cancel


================================================================================
3. RPC (Remote Procedure Call)
================================================================================

📌 API looks like calling a function

Example

POST /calculateTax
POST /sendOtp

Payload

{
  "amount": 1000
}

REST vs RPC (same requirement)
REST:  POST /taxes   or  GET /taxes?amount=1000
RPC:   POST /calculateTax

Modern RPC: gRPC (binary, fast, strongly typed with Protobuf) — used a lot for service-to-service.

✅ Simple logic mapping
❌ Not resource-oriented

Used in: Legacy systems, internal services, gRPC microservices


================================================================================
4. SOAP (Simple Object Access Protocol)
================================================================================

📌 XML-based, strict contract

Characteristics
- Uses WSDL (contract first)
- Strong typing
- Heavy XML
- Built-in standards for security (WS-Security), transactions

✅ Secure / formal contracts
❌ Slow, verbose

Used in: Banks, legacy enterprise systems


================================================================================
5. GraphQL
================================================================================

📌 Client decides what data it needs
📌 Usually ONE endpoint: POST /graphql

Example

{
  user(id: 10) {
    name
    email
  }
}

REST problem GraphQL solves
REST: GET /users/10 returns ALL fields (or you make many endpoints)
GraphQL: client asks only for name + email → no over-fetching

✅ No over-fetching
✅ Single endpoint
❌ Complex caching / N+1 query risk on backend

Used in: Modern frontend-heavy apps


================================================================================
6. Event-Driven API (Async Pattern)
================================================================================

📌 Communication via events (not waiting for HTTP response from other service)

Example

Order Service → OrderCreated event → Kafka Topic → Billing Service
                                              └→ Notification Service

Sync REST call:
Order Service --HTTP--> Billing Service (Order waits for reply)

Async event:
Order Service --publish--> Kafka (Order continues; Billing reacts later)

✅ Highly scalable
✅ Loose coupling
❌ Harder to debug / eventual consistency

Used in: Microservices, Kafka, RabbitMQ


================================================================================
7. Webhooks
================================================================================

📌 Server calls client on event (reverse of normal API call)

Normal API:  Client → Server
Webhook:     Server → Client (when something happens)

Example

Payment success → Provider calls your POST /webhook/payment

Must handle:
- Signature verification (is this really Stripe/PayPal?)
- Retries (same webhook may arrive twice → make handler idempotent)
- Fast ACK (return 200 quickly, process async if heavy)

✅ Real-time updates
❌ Retry handling needed


================================================================================
8. API Gateway Pattern
================================================================================

📌 Single entry point for multiple services

Responsibilities
- Authentication / Authorization
- Rate limiting
- Logging / tracing
- Routing / load balancing
- Sometimes request aggregation

Flow

Client → API Gateway → User Service
                     → Order Service
                     → Payment Service

Without gateway: client must know every microservice URL (bad)
With gateway: client talks to one host only

Tools
- Spring Cloud Gateway
- Kong
- NGINX


================================================================================
9. Backend-for-Frontend (BFF)
================================================================================

📌 Separate backend tailored per frontend

Example

Mobile App → Mobile BFF  (small JSON, fewer fields, maybe GraphQL)
Web App    → Web BFF     (richer data, more nested objects)
Both BFFs  → same microservices behind them

✅ Optimized responses per client
❌ More services to maintain


================================================================================
10. HATEOAS
================================================================================

📌 API responses contain links to related actions/resources
📌 Part of "true" REST maturity model (rarely fully used)

Example

{
  "id": 10,
  "links": {
    "self": "/users/10",
    "orders": "/users/10/orders"
  }
}

✅ Discoverable APIs
❌ Rarely used in practice (most teams just document APIs in Swagger/OpenAPI)


================================================================================
11. Pagination, Filtering & Sorting Pattern
================================================================================

📌 Handles large datasets — never return unbounded lists in production APIs

Example

GET /users?page=1&size=20&sort=name,asc&status=ACTIVE

Also common:
- Offset pagination: page + size
- Cursor / keyset pagination: ?cursor=abc123 (better for large data / infinite scroll)

Filtering:  ?status=ACTIVE&role=ADMIN
Sorting:    ?sort=createdAt,desc


================================================================================
12. Versioning Pattern
================================================================================

📌 Prevents breaking old clients when API changes

Common approaches

URL versioning (most common in Java shops):
/api/v1/users
/api/v2/users

Header versioning:
Accept: application/vnd.app.v1+json

Rule of thumb:
- Additive change (new optional field) → usually no new version
- Breaking change (rename/remove field, change meaning) → new version


